{"records":[{"id":"r46b2sgkkb","title":"IntS6 and the Integrator phosphatase module tune the efficiency of select premature transcription termination events. Fujiwara et al.","description":"The metazoan-specific Integrator complex catalyzes 3' end processing of small nuclear RNAs (snRNAs) and premature termination that attenuates transcription of many protein-coding genes. Integrator has RNA endonuclease and protein phosphatase activities, but it remains unclear if both are required for complex function. Here, we show IntS6 (Integrator subunit 6) over-expression blocks Integrator function at a subset of Drosophila protein-coding genes, while having no effect on snRNAs or attenuation of other loci. Over-expressed IntS6 titrates protein phosphatase 2A (PP2A) subunits, thereby only affecting gene loci where phosphatase activity is necessary for Integrator function. IntS6 functions analogous to a PP2A regulatory B subunit as over-expression of canonical B subunits, which do not bind Integrator, are also sufficient to inhibit Integrator activity. These results show that the phosphatase module is critical at only a subset of Integrator regulated genes and point to PP2A recruitment as a tunable step that modulates transcription termination efficiency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-10T13:41:25.195Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r46b2sgkkb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r46b2sgkkb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jeremy","last_name":"Wilusz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rina","last_name":"Fujiwara","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Si-Nan","last_name":"Zhai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dongming","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Tracey","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xu-Kai","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aayushi","last_name":"Shah","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Fields","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria Sarai","last_name":"Mendoza-Figueroa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Meline","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Deirdre","last_name":"Tatomer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"31925011"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RR210031"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grant_id":"R35-GM119735"}],"categories":["Transcription","RNA","Phosphatase","Termination of Transcription"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nvjmk7psf9","title":"CDK9-catalyzed phosphorylation of METTL3 promotes RNA polymerase II pause release and drives tumorigenesis","description":"CDK9-catalyzed phosphorylation of METTL3 promotes RNA polymerase II pause release and drives tumorigenesis: Precise control of RNA polymerase II (Pol II) pausing and release is essential for transcriptional regulation, yet how it is coordinated with oncogenic signaling and RNA modification remains unclear. Here, we identify CDK9-mediated phosphorylation of the m6A methyltransferase METTL3 as a key driver of Pol II pause release and oncogenic transcription. CDK9 extensively phosphorylates the intrinsically disordered N-terminal region of METTL3, promoting its incorporation into transcriptional condensates and its interactions with METTL14, Pol II and CDK9. This phosphorylation enables co-transcriptional m6A deposition and accelerates Pol II pause release, particularly at immediate early genes. Genetic or pharmacologic disruption of this CDK9-METTL3 phosphorylation impairs m6A deposition on mRNAs and chromatin-associated RNAs, delays transcriptional activation, and suppresses leukemogenesis in vitro and in vivo. Together, these findings define a CDK9-METTL3-m6A axis that mechanistically links kinase signaling to epitranscriptomic remodeling and transcriptional dynamics and reveal a therapeutically actionable vulnerability in m6A-dependent cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-07T09:04:36.308Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nvjmk7psf9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nvjmk7psf9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"WEI","last_name":"LI","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Image Acquisition"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9npznfkhsj","title":"Original Data of Tripartite phosphorylation of SPT5 by CDK9 times pause release and tunes elongation rate of RNA polymerase II by Sun and Fisher ","description":"The RNA polymerase II (RNAPII) transcription cycle is regulated throughout its duration by protein phosphorylation. Previously, two regions phosphorylated by cyclin-dependent kinase 9 (CDK9) in elongation factor SPT5—the linker between KOWx-4 and KOW5 domains and carboxy-terminal repeat (CTR) 1—were implicated in promoter-proximal pausing and termination, respectively. Here we show that phosphorylations in the linker, CTR1 and a third region, CTR2, coordinately control pause release, elongation speed and termination. Pausing was unaffected or increased by mutations preventing CTR1 or CTR2 phosphorylation, respectively, but attenuated when both CTRs were mutated. Whereas loss of CTR1 phosphorylation slowed elongation and repressed nascent transcription, simultaneous CTR2 mutation partially reversed both effects. Nevertheless, mutating both CTRs had additive effects on splicing, termination, steady-state mRNA levels and cell proliferation. Therefore, tripartite SPT5 phosphorylation times pause release and tunes RNAPII elongation rate to ensure productive transcription and cell viability. This file contains the unprocessed and uncompressed imaging data.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-08T07:16:23.512Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9npznfkhsj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9npznfkhsj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R35 GM127289 "}],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n4dccm9r9h","title":"SPT5 stabilizes RNA polymerase II, orchestrates transcription cycles, and maintains the enhancer landscape","description":"Transcription progression is governed by multitasking regulators including SPT5,\nan evolutionarily conserved factor implicated in virtually all transcriptional steps from\nenhancer activation to termination. Here we utilize a rapid degradation system and\nreveal crucial functions of SPT5 in maintaining cellular and chromatin RNA polymerase\nII (Pol II) levels. Rapid SPT5 depletion causes a pronounced reduction of paused Pol II\nat promoters and enhancers, distinct from NELF degradation resulting in short-distance\npaused Pol II redistribution. Most genes exhibit downregulation but not upregulation,\naccompanied by greatly impaired transcription activation, altered chromatin landscape\nat enhancers, and severe Pol II processivity defects at gene bodies. Phosphorylation of\nan SPT5 linker at serine 666 potentiates pause release and is antagonized by\nIntegrator-PP2A (INTAC) targeting SPT5 and Pol II, while phosphorylation of SPT5 \nC-terminal region links to 3' end termination. Our findings position SPT5 as an\nessential positive regulator of global transcription.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-10T13:08:36.278Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n4dccm9r9h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n4dccm9r9h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shibin","last_name":"hu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"linna","last_name":"peng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"fei","last_name":"chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ypkpxp6rtz","title":"Uncropped blots for \"Sequential verification of transcription by Integrator and Restrictor\"","description":"Uncropped western blots for \"Sequential verification of transcription by Integrator and Restrictor\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-21T15:14:50.347Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ypkpxp6rtz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ypkpxp6rtz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Steven","last_name":"West","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dnryw89c68","title":"RNA-seq data from paper \"Coagulation factor FVII fine-tunes hepatic steatosis by blocking AKT-CD36-mediated fatty acid uptake\"","description":"RNA-seq data from paper \"Coagulation factor FVII fine-tunes hepatic steatosis by blocking AKT-CD36-mediated fatty acid uptake\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-22T08:36:30.88Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dnryw89c68","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dnryw89c68"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yao","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junli","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suzhen","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5sdnbktpg6","title":"A study of: Selective PP2A enhancement through biased heterotrimer stabilization. Leonard D, Huang W, et al.","description":"Here we define the structural and biological basis by which a class of small molecules (lead compound denoted DT-061), selectively binds and stabilizes a single B-subunit containing Protein Phosphatase 2A (PP2A) heterotrimer to drive the targeted dephosphorylation of select PP2A substrates that include c-MYC. This work provides structural and molecular insights into PP2A holoenzyme regulation, identifies a new therapeutic strategy for protein complex targeting and activation, and presents a basis for phosphatase activating therapeutics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-26T22:53:06.941Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5sdnbktpg6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5sdnbktpg6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Leonard","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Structural Biology","Cancer","Protein Phosphatase","Therapeutics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hvgpbgpjfw","title":"Gene set showing overlapping of ZMYND8 and CDK9 in 293T cells","description":"This data set represents the overlapping genes that show co-presence of ZMYND8 and CDK9 at the TSS region as mentioned in Ghosh et al. with manuscript title: \"Positive regulation of transcription by human ZMYND8 through its  association with P-TEFb complex\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-27T10:23:13.45Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hvgpbgpjfw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hvgpbgpjfw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Debabrata","last_name":"Biswas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rjmwtfj6jb","title":"Dataset for: Can the targeted source information be extracted from interfered magnetic anomaly?","description":"Dataset and function for reading the .grd files in Matlab. The synthetic magnetic data, extracted magnetic data, and residual magnetic data for manuscript 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 3.1, 3.2, 3.3 and Supporting Information S1, S5, S6, S7, S8, S9, S10, and S11.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-24T16:55:15.852Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rjmwtfj6jb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rjmwtfj6jb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dan","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Applied Geophysics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f6zw5bzfhc","title":"Dephosphorylation of bZIP59 by PP2A ensures appropriate shade avoidance response in Arabidopsis","description":"Changes in light quality and quantity experienced by many shade-intolerant plants grown in close proximity lead to transcriptional reprogramming and shade avoidance syndrome (SAS). Despite the importance of phosphorylation-dependent signaling in cellular physiology, phosphorylation events during SAS are largely unknown. Here, we examined shade-regulated phosphorylation events in Arabidopsis using quantitative phosphoproteomics. We confirmed shade-induced dephosphorylation of bZIP59, a basic region/leucine zipper motif (bZIP) transcription factor. Shade treatment promotes the nuclear localization of bZIP59, which can be mimicked by mutation of the phosphorylation sites on bZIP59. Phenotypic analysis identified that bZIP59 negatively regulated shade-induced hypocotyl elongation. bZIP59 repressed the shade-induced activation of certain growth-related genes, while shade increased the DNA binding of bZIP59. Furthermore, the protein phosphatase 2A (PP2A) mediated de-phosphorylation of bZIP59. Our study characterized a previously unidentified mechanism by which the phyB-PP2A-bZIP59 regulatory module integrates shade signals and transcriptomes, broadening our knowledge of phosphorylation strategies for rapid adaptation to shade.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-04T14:47:30.994Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f6zw5bzfhc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f6zw5bzfhc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lin","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139021,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":167,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139021,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138246,"restricted":70,"embargoed":705},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2335},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":701},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":480},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":404},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":337},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":229}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136364},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1908},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":140},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":